From: hawkff <109485367+hawkff@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 21:21:04 +0000
Subject: Add a flag to run WebAssembly in the interpreter when JIT is blocked

A renderer for a site with JIT blocked gets --jitless. V8 then hides the
WebAssembly object, so pages that need WebAssembly fail on those sites.

Add the WebAssemblyInterpreterWithoutJit feature, off by default, with an
entry on the flags page. When it is on, renderers with JIT blocked also
get --wasm-jitless, except PDF renderers. V8 then keeps the WebAssembly
object and runs every module in its WebAssembly interpreter. The
interpreter translates modules into its own bytecode and executes it with
handlers that are part of the browser binary, so the renderer still
generates no machine code for WebAssembly. A V8 build without the
interpreter ignores the flag, and WebAssembly stays unavailable there.
With the feature off, nothing changes.

The interpreter is much slower than compiled code, and compiling a module
blocks the page while it runs. It supports a subset of WebAssembly:
modules that use the newer exception handling instructions (exnref) fail
to compile, and JavaScript Promise Integration is not available. V8
treats the interpreter as an experimental feature.

Also remove commented-out code that would have kept the JIT compilers for
WebAssembly on these sites.

